#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183943 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184322 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a85fece-e40f-543c-b3c3-ae43a5175808" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient is Negative One: Odd Linear Terms"}] :question/id #uuid "9ef9f522-9802-5d91-8610-1341722d7920" :question/math-academy-id "e-1429" :question/problem "Rewrite $-x^2 +7x -13$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-(x^2 -7x +13)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\nx^2 -7x+13\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 -{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}x +{\\color{purple}{13}}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* $\\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}\\right)^2,$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{13}}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 -{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}x + \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}\\right)^2} -\\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}\\right)^2+ {\\color{purple}{13}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x-  {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}\\right)^2  -\\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}\\right)^2 + {\\color{purple}{13}}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x-  {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}\\right)^2  +\\dfrac{3}4.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+7x-13 & =-({(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}-{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183944 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184323 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "564f2cf7-aaaf-5dea-b619-a560c3151fcc" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "b38247c0-cb0a-5fa0-afed-45c7e1ba9e3c" :question/math-academy-id "e-1430" :question/problem "Rewrite $2x^2 - 6x+1$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, we take out a factor of $2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n2\\left(x^2 - 3x + \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\nx^2 - 3x + {\\color{purple} \\dfrac{1}{2} }\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2-{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}x +  {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{1}{2}}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* $\\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}\\right)^2,$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple} \\dfrac{1}{2} }$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2-{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}x + \\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}\\right)^2} - \\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}\\right)^2+ {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{1}{2}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x - \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2 - \\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}\\right)^2+ {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{1}{2}}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x - \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2 - \\dfrac{7}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}-3x+\\frac{1}{2}) & =2({(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{7}{4}) \\\\  & =2{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{7}{4} \\\\  & =2{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184115 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184211 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "808c9f7b-b951-5306-bce8-efc1aec8c2b7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient Is Negative One"}] :question/id #uuid "10318f8d-0396-53de-af20-e3b9ee6ac3d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-9932" :question/problem "Rewrite $-x^2+6x+1$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-(x^2 - 6x -1)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\nx^2 - 6x - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{3}}x - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nThen, we add and then subtract ${\\color{blue}{3}}^2,$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{1}}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{3}}x + {\\color{blue}{3}}^2} - {\\color{blue}{3}}^2 - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-{\\color{blue}{3}})^2 - {\\color{blue}{3}}^2- {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-{\\color{blue}{3}})^2 - 10\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+6x+1 & =-((x-3)^{2}-10) \\\\  & =-(x-3)^{2}+10 \\\\  & =10-(x-3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184116 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184212 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3202d7cc-541b-553b-8ec1-d4ea85dd0590" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "8b469eb3-6577-588c-aa5b-7d168ea6a0dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-9933" :question/problem "Rewrite $-2x^2+20x - 40$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-2.$ So, we take out a factor of $-2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-2\\left(x^2 - 10x + 20\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\nx^2 - 10x  + {\\color{purple}20}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}2}$ as a factor: \n\n$$\nx^2- {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue} 5}\\cdot x + {\\color{purple}20}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${\\color{blue} 5}^2,$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}20}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2- {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue} 5}\\cdot x + {\\color{blue} 5}^2} - {\\color{blue} 5}^2 + {\\color{purple}20}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x-5\\right)^2 -{\\color{blue} 5}^2 + {\\color{purple}20}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x-5\\right)^2 - 5.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2({x}^{2}-10x+20) & =-2({(x-5)}^{2}-5) \\\\  & =-2{(x-5)}^{2}+(-2)\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =-2{(x-5)}^{2}+10\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184448 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184211 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "808c9f7b-b951-5306-bce8-efc1aec8c2b7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient Is Negative One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05eadef5-d4bf-541e-bd2e-343c29f59c27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56-(k-8)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244361} {:answer/id #uuid "626efd7c-bc12-5b9b-928b-743c558ef6e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "46-(k-8)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245177} {:answer/id #uuid "7b99c3dc-ae1f-5598-a413-19b87a0bf35c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42-(k+8)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245397} {:answer/id #uuid "90fc842e-e24a-567f-8bf4-a2a2a63772a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "38-(k+8)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245580} {:answer/id #uuid "a1c838f7-516c-56c5-9e1d-df6702452695" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54-(k-8)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05eadef5-d4bf-541e-bd2e-343c29f59c27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56-(k-8)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244361} :answer-field/id #uuid "7819a661-bbd8-5b64-a989-336243949373" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246755}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7b217693-bc6b-565e-8cf8-4ff0d3fc9863" :question/math-academy-id "q-112300" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({k}^{2}-16k+8)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{k}^{2}-16k+8\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{k}^{2}-2\\cdot 8k+8\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${8}^{2},$ writing the constant term $8$ last:\n\n$$\n{{k}^{2}-2\\cdot 8k+{8}^{2}}_{⏟}-{8}^{2}+8\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(k-8)^{2}-{8}^{2}+8\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(k-8)^{2}-56.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({k}^{2}-16k+8) & =-({(k-8)}^{2}-56) \\\\  & =-(k-8)^{2}+56 \\\\  & =56-(k-8)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272052 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184211 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "808c9f7b-b951-5306-bce8-efc1aec8c2b7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient Is Negative One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "059f6a26-1b29-519e-a543-69be7ed1b4d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16-(x+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269318} {:answer/id #uuid "9f60c504-aeae-5275-9938-cb7e27e9ec18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8-(x-2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270685} {:answer/id #uuid "c9ada0ec-df6d-5400-b1fa-9d1ba5b69efd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-(x-2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271041} {:answer/id #uuid "fb114b87-c749-5289-8848-51e528face0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12-(x+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271466} {:answer/id #uuid "ff2a8a1e-0990-533c-8797-de1d653da457" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12-(x+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271501}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "059f6a26-1b29-519e-a543-69be7ed1b4d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16-(x+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269318} :answer-field/id #uuid "537b2e4e-ba82-5a3e-9315-02cb6492d8f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271661}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e832f16e-81bf-5dd5-9dcf-748202928d1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-140318" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({x}^{2}+4x-12)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+4x-12\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x-12\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${2}^{2},$ writing the constant term $12$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2}}_{⏟}-{2}^{2}-12\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+2)^{2}-{2}^{2}-12\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+2)^{2}-16.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}+4x-12) & =-({(x+2)}^{2}-16) \\\\  & =-{(x+2)}^{2}+16 \\\\  & =16-(x+2)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272058 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184322 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a85fece-e40f-543c-b3c3-ae43a5175808" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient is Negative One: Odd Linear Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "071c98e7-f045-5699-97e6-8ca98ff4c425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{89}{4}-{(x+14)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269336} {:answer/id #uuid "0b6551d7-ff9d-5fcb-9ba8-47eb76d9737a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269367} {:answer/id #uuid "92214c5a-0fb3-5f9c-b2fd-8c3a954013bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "59-{(x+14)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270546} {:answer/id #uuid "b1f6b651-8575-5bc8-a027-fe8e7ce11c40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270861} {:answer/id #uuid "d730e81c-c1fd-5d3a-9ed2-255c3dad533b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{49}{4}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271160}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b6551d7-ff9d-5fcb-9ba8-47eb76d9737a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269367} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e101ba8-4e79-5217-9a66-13f1e2c2b19e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271788}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5f049fbc-7296-56e3-bb5f-3d51d2204d7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-140330" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({x}^{2}+7x+10)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+7x+10\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+10\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $10$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}+7x+10) & =-({(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{9}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184483 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184322 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a85fece-e40f-543c-b3c3-ae43a5175808" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient is Negative One: Odd Linear Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ab1fc57-4376-575b-9d81-bd754999d008" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{49}{4} - (y + \\frac{5}{2})^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181076} {:answer/id #uuid "e1ba29b7-1b53-58cd-951b-d7c6e5462caf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ide